      "review_description": "If you do not use the power when it is produced you lose it to the grid for a small amount of compensation.   I use a hybrid system using solar tubes for hot water and solar panels for electricity. The solar tubes are excellent generating hot water for most of the year, storage is obviously included.  However I would assume that if you used your solar panels to heat the water and had the hot water cylinder on a time switch, your wastage back to the grid would be negligible.  The solar tubes produce the equivalent of 1.5kwh.  The feed in meter gives you the total of unused power fed into the grid from the panels, and the charged power taken from the grid.  Which is basically everything you need to know.  Power production on a dull day is negligible, hot water production is less affected.",
      "value_for_money_rating": 4.0,
